+export PATH := /home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew/bin:$(PATH)
+
+.PHONY: build install acceptance test ui-test flatpak flatpak-lint guest provision clean
+
+build:
+	cd . && CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -trimpath -ldflags="-s -w" -o bin/phial .
+
+install: build
+	./bin/phial install .
+
+acceptance: build
+	./bin/phial acceptance
+
+test:
+	cd . && go test ./internal/...
+
+ui-test: build
+	./tests/ui-test.sh
+
+flatpak-lint:
+	python3 -c "import json; json.load(open('flatpak/com.pojtinger.felicitas.Phial.json')); print('manifest ok')"
+
+flatpak: build flatpak-lint
+	flatpak-builder --force-clean --repo=flatpak/repo flatpak/build flatpak/com.pojtinger.felicitas.Phial.json
+	flatpak build-bundle flatpak/repo flatpak/phial.flatpak com.pojtinger.felicitas.Phial
+
+guest:
+	@echo "The guest is provisioned by the vm.provision MCP tool."
+
+provision: build
+	./bin/phial vm provision phial
+
+clean:
+	rm -f bin/phial
diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 311d738b2bc21d0f9c6f2d7025b03273c7a87bfb..a72e9485ebe40a97f3d6e1055fbb638f43a2cb6b 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -1,10 +1,207 @@
 # Phial
 
-Run a VM that keeps your network, devices, and secrets.
+Phial runs a Fedora Workstation 45 guest under QEMU on a Fedora Workstation 45 host. The host keeps every secret. The guest receives time-boxed access to the secrets that the host user grants.
+
+The host is trusted. The guest is not trusted. Read [ROADMAP.md](ROADMAP.md) for the full design and the threat model.
+
+## Terms
+
+- Env mode means that the guest reads a secret from a file.
+- Proxy mode means that the host injects a secret into an outbound request.
+- A grant is a time box on one secret.
+- The launcher is the script that builds the QEMU argument list.
+- The mapping file lists the providers in four columns: name, mode, source, upstream.
+- A share is a host directory that the guest mounts over virtiofs.
+
+## Requirements
+
+- Fedora Workstation 45 on the host.
+- Homebrew for the user space tools. The Brewfile is the manifest.
+- DNF for the tools that Homebrew does not provide on Linux. Read [docs/TOOLCHAIN.md](docs/TOOLCHAIN.md).
+- Root once through run0 for the host setup bundle.
+
+## Install
+
+Install the user space tools:
+
+```sh
+brew bundle install
+```
+
+Build the static CLI and install phial into your user directories:
+
+```sh
+make install
+```
+
+Apply the root setup bundle:
+
+```sh
+run0 ./host/phial-host-root-setup.sh
+```
+
+If SELinux blocks the execution, pipe the script in instead: run0 bash -s < ./host/phial-host-root-setup.sh
+
+Open KeePassXC and unlock the database. Keep the KeePassXC window open and unlocked, so its Secret Service answers one keepassxc:// lookup with no prompt.
+
+Edit the launcher variables at the top of the script:
+
+```sh
+xdg-open ~/.config/phial/phial-vm.sh
+```
+
+Edit the mapping file:
+
+```sh
+xdg-open ~/.config/phial/mapping
+```
+
+## The application
+
+Start Phial with no argument to open the window:
+
+```sh
+phial
+```
+
+The window carries a sidebar with three pages. The pages are Machines, Secrets, and Requests.
+
+The Machines page lists every machine. Each row carries a name, a state, and a control. Select a row to open the machine page.
+
+The machine page carries the state, the USB settings, the display settings, and the security settings. The header carries the primary action and the delete control.
+
+Press the list control in the header to open the Progress panel. The panel lists every install step. The panel reports the download size and the package count. The panel also carries Cancel Install. Cancel stops the installer and removes the partial disk.
+
+Quit asks for confirmation while a machine runs. The confirmation stops every running machine. Close the window to keep Phial in the background instead.
+
+The application also holds the agent channel open while the window stays closed.
+
+## The agent channel
+
+Phial serves a Model Context Protocol endpoint on two sockets:
+
+- `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/phial/mcp.sock` serves the host.
+- `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/phial/mcp-guest.sock` serves the guest.
+
+The guest reaches the host endpoint through vsock port 8080 at `http://host.internal:8080/mcp`.
+
+Every action in the window has a matching tool. List the tools with the `tools/list` request.
+
+## Flatpak
+
+The Flatpak manifest sits at flatpak/com.pojtinger.felicitas.Phial.json. It builds QEMU, virtiofsd, passt, swtpm, socat, Caddy, waypipe, FreeRDP, edk2, and Phial itself from source. Run make flatpak. Read flatpak/README.md.
+
+## The virtual machine
+
+Start the guest:
+
+```sh
+systemctl --user start phial-vm.service
+```
+
+The unit phial-vm.service calls the launcher. The launcher reads its variables from the top of the script. Set PHIAL_SECURE_BOOT=off in the script only when a Machine Owner Key cannot be enrolled. A Machine Owner Key is a key that shim trusts for custom modules. Set PHIAL_GPU to venus, virgl, or none. venus turns on blob, venus, and the native context as the roadmap asks. Read docs/TOOLCHAIN.md when the host 3D stack misbehaves.
+
+The desktop entries open the unit and the script in the default editor. The display switch entry swaps between egl-headless and GTK for the next run. Use the GTK display for early boot, for the firmware, and for MokManager.
+
+## Install the guest
+
+Run the installer helper:
+
+```sh
+./guest/install-guest.sh
+```
+
+The helper downloads the Fedora 45 netinst assets, packs [guest/phial.ks](guest/phial.ks) into a FAT image, and boots the installer with the kickstart through shim for the full Secure Boot chain. The serial console streams to a log, so you can watch the installer work. The kickstart installs the workstation environment, creates the user phial, sets the hostname phial, installs the SSH keys from https://github.com/pojntfx.keys, and enables sshd and qemu-guest-agent.
+
+Provision the guest after the first boot:
+
+```sh
+make guest
+```
+
+The Ansible playbooks follow the layout of vanlug-infrastructure, with kickstart instead of cloud-init. The guest is a pet machine. Run the playbooks again at any time.
+
+## Secrets and grants
+
+Add a row to the mapping file, then materialize every row:
+
+```sh
+phial sync
+```
+
+Grant one secret for one hour:
+
+```sh
+phial allow --for 1h openai
+```
+
+Show the remaining time:
+
+```sh
+phial status
+```
+
+Revoke a grant early:
+
+```sh
+phial revoke openai
+```
+
+List the secret names, never the values:
+
+```sh
+phial ls
+```
+
+Env rows land as files in the secrets share. The guest mounts the share read-only at /mnt/phial-secrets. The runner loads every file as an environment variable:
+
+```sh
+phial run env
+```
+
+Proxy rows stay on the host. The guest calls http://host.internal:4000/<name> with the placeholder token phial-proxy-placeholder. Caddy matches the grant file for the row, injects the Authorization header, and forwards the request to the upstream. A missing grant file returns 403. An unmatched path returns 404.
+
+Grant subject types are api-key, ssh-key, and gpg-key. An SSH key grant loads the key from ~/.config/phial/keys into the dedicated agent with a one hour lifetime and a confirmation prompt. The prompt appears on the host through the GTK askpass. The GnuPG prompt appears on the host through pinentry-gnome3. The guest holds no private key.
+
+## Display and audio
+
+- Use phial-waypipe <command> for one app over waypipe on vsock. The guest display is 0.
+- Use phial-rdp for a full desktop session over RDP through the gnome-remote-desktop system daemon. Audio and clipboard go over RDP.
+- Attach to the serial console with socat - UNIX-CONNECT:$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/phial/vm/console.sock for kernel work.
 
-## Overview
+## Networking and devices
 
-🚧 This project is a work-in-progress! Instructions will be added as soon as it is usable. 🚧
+The root bundle creates the bridge phialbr0, adds the Ethernet port to it, and owns the tap phialtap0 for the guest. The passt fallback route has the highest guest route metric. Prefer IPv6 on every interface. The dual-stack path is a fallback only.
+
+The launcher passes the named USB devices with usb-host by vendor and product ID. To pass the second xHCI controller with its whole hub, set PHIAL_USB_XHCI in the launcher and bind the controller:
+
+```sh
+run0 ./host/phial-vfio.sh bind 0000:00:14.0
+```
+
+Restore the host drivers when the VM stops:
+
+```sh
+run0 ./host/phial-vfio.sh restore 0000:00:14.0
+```
+
+Never bind the only xHCI controller to the guest. The launcher refuses when the host has one controller.
+
+## Acceptance
+
+Run the host acceptance suite:
+
+```sh
+make acceptance
+```
+
+Run the CLI and proxy test suite:
+
+```sh
+make test
+```
+
+Read [docs/VALIDATION.md](docs/VALIDATION.md) for the validation results of this tree.
 
 ## License
 
