@@ -279,3 +282,264 @@ - Point the agent at the guest serial console for kernel work.
 - Point the agent at the waypipe path for single apps and at the RDP path for the full session.
 - Acceptance: a guest agent can consume a granted secret for an hour, and it cannot add a provider or extend a grant.
 
+## Shell removal
+
+- Remove every shell script except the kickstart and the systemd units.
+- A script that must exist becomes a small Go subcommand or a function in the main binary.
+- pinentry-gnome3 replaces the GTK askpass script for ssh-add confirmation prompts.
+- The acceptance run, the installer, the root setup, the vfio helper, the waypipe helper, the RDP helper, the guest forward dispatcher, and the guest runner all become Go.
+- Acceptance: the tree holds no .sh files outside guest/phial.ks and the unit files.
+
+## Host application
+
+- Ship one static Go binary with no cgo.
+- Build the GUI with puregotk and libadwaita. Follow the GNOME Human Interface Guidelines.
+- Use a sidebar for top level navigation. Use boxed lists for settings lists. Use header bar buttons for page actions.
+- Use action dialogs to create items. Use placeholders for empty states. Use toasts for non-critical errors. Use confirmation only for irreversible actions.
+- The GUI drives one MCP daemon for every state change. It never edits state files behind the daemon.
+- One systemd user unit runs the daemon. The daemon serves MCP over Streamable HTTP on a unix socket in the runtime directory.
+- The guest reaches the same MCP over the vsock forward as http://host.internal:8080/mcp.
+- The MCP tools cover every feature: secrets, grants, virtual machines, forwards, USB, display, approvals, and system setup status.
+- Acceptance: one static binary runs the GUI, and the daemon serves every MCP tool.
+
+## Requests and approvals
+
+- The guest agent calls the MCP on the host for actions, for example a new redirect or a grant request.
+- Actions that add a provider or open a grant wait for host approval. The threat model from the earlier phase applies.
+- The GUI lists pending requests with Approve and Deny controls. A desktop notification announces each new request.
+- Do not show a dialog without a user action. Show the request in the requests view and in a notification.
+- A pending request expires after five minutes and then counts as denied.
+- Acceptance: a guest request appears in the GUI and in a notification, and the grant opens only after the host approves.
+
+## Secrets with KeePassXC
+
+- Keep the Secret Service as the store. The GUI resolves keepassxc:// rows for display names and never shows values.
+- The GUI can write a test entry into the store, so the store path gets exercised end to end.
+- Add, edit, and remove providers through action dialogs in the GUI.
+- Acceptance: a provider created in the GUI resolves through the Secret Service and grants from the GUI.
+
+## Virtual machines
+
+- Hold multiple VM definitions as JSON files in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/phial/vms.
+- One definition carries the name, the CPU count, the memory size, the disk path and size, the display mode, the GPU mode, the Secure Boot switch, the USB list, the xHCI controller, and the network mode.
+- The GUI manages the lifecycle: create, start, stop, restart, install from the kickstart, and delete with confirmation.
+- The launcher builds the QEMU argument list in Go per definition. The template unit phial-vm@.service runs the VM in the foreground.
+- Manage USB in the GUI: the named device list, the second xHCI controller selection, and bind or restore through run0.
+- Manage display in the GUI: the mode switch, the GPU mode, waypipe app launch, RDP open, and serial attach.
+- Acceptance: two VMs with different definitions run at the same time, and their USB and display settings apply.
+
+## Flatpak
+
+- Ship one manifest, com.pojtinger.felicitas.Phial.json, on the GNOME Platform runtime with the Go SDK extension.
+- Build every dependency into the Flatpak: the app, QEMU, virtiofsd, passt, swtpm, socat, Caddy, FreeRDP, waypipe, libsecret, and edk2 for the OVMF firmware.
+- Grant wayland, x11 fallback, ipc, dri, kvm, device all for vfio and vhost-vsock, network share, session bus, system bus for systemd and polkit, and the secrets and notifications talk names.
+- Keep system setup host side. Huge pages, udev rules, the bridge, and the DNF tools need root. The GUI offers one button that runs run0 for them, so polkit asks the user.
+- Write the submission metadata: metainfo.xml, the desktop file, icon.svg and icon-symbolic.svg, and the doap file.
+- Acceptance: the manifest passes flatpak-builder-lint, and the app builds and runs from the Flatpak.
+
+## Data locations and portals
+
+- Keep definitions and the mapping in $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/phial.
+- Keep disks and install assets in $XDG_DATA_HOME/phial.
+- Keep sockets, the grants, the secrets share, the MCP socket, and the runtime state in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/phial.
+- Use portals whenever one exists for the job: notifications, the file chooser, and the screenshot portal.
+- Use direct D-Bus for systemd and for the Secret Service, because no portal covers them.
+
+## Milestones for this phase
+
+- M1: the roadmap holds this phase.
+- M2: the core is Go and the shell scripts are gone.
+- M3: the MCP daemon, the guest forward, and the approvals work.
+- M4: the GUI works with the GNOME HIG.
+- M5: the Flatpak manifest and the metadata pass lint.
+- M6: everything validates end to end and the docs match.
+
+
+## UI polish and correctness
+
+- Fix suffix icon buttons in list rows so they do not change row height on hover. Use flat icon buttons with a fixed size.
+- Fix the secrets page delete button and grant button height. Use compact suffix controls.
+- Use a pre-made searchable list component for the guest app browser. Filter by name as the user types.
+- Fix the serial console action so it finds a terminal emulator on the host. Try kgx, gnome-terminal, and xterm in order.
+- Keep the machines list as action rows. Do not replace the New Machine row with a pill button.
+- Fix the mobile sidebar so tapping a row shows that page. Use ConnectRowActivated with proper row identity.
+- Make VM detail pages real navigation pages so the user can swipe back. Push them onto the Adw.NavigationView.
+- Remove Run Acceptance from the settings dialog. It is a developer tool, not a user setting.
+- Merge USB devices and hub controller into one Preferences section. Merge Display and GPU into one section. Merge Secure Boot into a Security section.
+- Replace the hub controller text entry with a selectable row. Show available PCI xHCI controllers and let the user pick one or choose none.
+- Make non-representable states non-representable. Do not show Start on a VM that has no installed system. Do not show Stop on a stopped VM.
+- Label the Run App action as waypipe in the guest. The user should know the app renders on the host.
+- Show a progress bar in the VM detail while an installer runs. Use a GTK progress bar or spinner with text.
+- Show every action error in a dialog or toast with full context. Make the error text selectable and copyable.
+- Report async errors from any action with the same error dialog. Do not log-only errors.
+- Toasts that need a VM restart must include a Restart button. Use sentence case with no trailing dots.
+
+## Storage and lifecycle
+
+- Delete old VM definitions and disks before creating new ones when storage is low. Warn the user first.
+- Track disk usage and surface it in the machine detail view.
+
+## End to end verification
+
+- Drive every UI action through the MCP and verify the result. Every button must have a matching MCP tool.
+- Test the full flow on desktop and mobile widths. Verify the sidebar collapse and page navigation.
+- Test the app browser with a real guest. Verify the desktop database listing and launch.
+- Test the installer with a progress bar. Verify the error dialog on failure.
+- Test every dialog and confirm the button hierarchy and mnemonic underlines are correct.
+
+## Detail page structure
+
+- Build the detail page as an Adw.NavigationPage with its own Adw.ToolbarView and Adw.HeaderBar. Follow the senbara pattern exactly.
+- The detail header bar must have a back button at start, the machine name as window title, and window controls at end.
+- The detail page must push onto the Adw.NavigationView so the user can swipe back or click the back button.
+- The sidebar must not show when a detail page is open. The detail page covers the full window.
+
+## Impossible states
+
+- Do not show Install and Start at the same time. A machine that is not installed shows only Install. A machine that is installed and stopped shows Start and Install. A machine that is running shows Stop and Restart.
+- Do not show Stop or Restart unless the machine is running.
+- Do not show console actions (Run App, RDP, Serial Console) unless the machine is running.
+- Do not show Delete while an install is running. Show only the progress bar.
+- Treat a machine with a disk larger than 1 GB as installed. Treat a machine with an empty disk as not installed.
+
+## Hub controller selection
+
+- Replace the hub controller text entry with an Adw.ComboRow dropdown. List available xHCI controllers from usb.listXhci. Include a None option.
+- The dropdown must show the PCI address and controller name for each option.
+- Bind and Restore buttons must be disabled or hidden when no hub controller is selected.
+
+## Toast spam
+
+- Do not show a toast when the user navigates between pages. Only show toasts for actual state changes (grant, revoke, install, delete, settings change).
+- Reduce toast noise from periodic refreshes. Never show a toast from a background refresh.
+
+## UI verification
+
+- Drive every page and dialog with real actions. Capture a screenshot of each.
+- Verify the sidebar shows only 3 items (Machines, Secrets, Requests) and that clicking each one navigates correctly.
+- Verify the detail page has proper back button and window controls.
+- Verify impossible states are not shown. Check every button visibility for every machine state.
+- Verify the hub controller dropdown lists real controllers.
+- Verify toasts only appear for real actions, not navigation.
+
