index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..4f59ff393514e48b5652c591f328ebc7a409dd45
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/VALIDATION.md
@@ -0,0 +1,191 @@
+# Validation
+
+This file records what phial validated on 2026-10-07 and 2026-10-08. The development host is a Lenovo ThinkPad X13 with an Intel Core Ultra 7 165U. It has 12 physical cores, 14 threads, and 30 GiB RAM. It runs Fedora Workstation 45 prerelease with kernel 7.2.6-300 and QEMU 11.1.0 rc3. The connected hardware is one USB hub chain with dummy devices (FTDI 0403:6001, CH340 1a86:55d3, MACROSILICON 345f:2131, USBRelay 16c0:05df, ASIX 0b95:1790). A USB LAN adapter (Realtek 0bda:8156) sits on the first xHCI controller. The host WLAN carries the host network. No USB Wi-Fi adapter exists yet, so the Wi-Fi passthrough path stays untested.
+
+## Host acceptance
+
+phial-acceptance runs every host acceptance item. All 30 items pass. This covers the display backends, the device list, the machine properties, the Rust virtiofsd, and the passt netdev. It also covers the DER enrollment flags of virt-fw-vars, the OVMF secboot pair, and the firmware descriptor with requires-smm.
+
+One note: this QEMU build accepts kernel-irqchip=split but does not list it in the q35,help output. The acceptance item therefore falls back to a probe boot and passes.
+
+## VM launch acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "the guest reaches the firmware, then the installer".
+
+- The firmware runs: the OVMF debug console log shows the UEFI phase, and the kernel then starts with Secure Boot enabled.
+- The installer runs: the serial console shows the Anaconda services, and the kickstart installs all 1856 packages to completion.
+
+The launcher builds the QEMU argument list in code. It uses q35, KVM, smm=on, kernel-irqchip=split, and the host CPU model. It gives the guest 11 vCPUs in one socket with threads=1. It backs the RAM with a shared memfd and huge pages, sets -overcommit mem-lock=on, and keeps the RTC on the host clock. It adds the OVMF secboot code and vars, TPM 2.0 with tpm-crb, and vsock with guest-cid=3. It adds the guest agent on a virtio serial port and two virtiofs devices with the tags hostshare and phial-secrets. It pins one iothread with num-queues=4 on the disk and adds a serial console on every run.
+
+Three fixes came out of this phase and are in the tree. The launcher waits for a live listener on the swtpm and virtiofsd sockets before it starts QEMU. virtiofsd restarts after a client disconnect. -overcommit mem-lock=on needs LimitMEMLOCK=infinity in the unit, and the root bundle raises the hard limit for the user manager.
+
+The Secure Boot chain has three parts. The firmware is OVMF secboot with smm=on. The installer kernel boots through shim with -shim, and the installed guest boots its signed Fedora kernel under Secure Boot. mokutil reports SecureBoot enabled in the guest.
+
+## Guest install acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "the guest boots under Secure Boot, and ssh phial reaches it".
+
+- The guest boots under Secure Boot: mokutil --sb-state in the guest prints SecureBoot enabled.
+- ssh phial reaches it: the host entry ssh phial connects over vsock with no network device, and the guest hostname phial answers.
+
+The install flow downloads the Fedora 45 netinst assets, packs guest/phial.ks into a FAT image with mtools, and boots the installer with the kickstart. The kickstart installs the workstation environment and creates the user phial with uid and gid 1000. It sets the hostname phial and installs the SSH keys from https://github.com/pojntfx.keys. It enables sshd and qemu-guest-agent, and it sets the default target to graphical.
+
+The guest agent answers from the first boot. A guest-ping over the QGA socket passes before provisioning.
+
+## Control plane acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "SSH works with no NIC other than loopback".
+
+- The guest runs one unit that exposes SSH over vsock with socat. It listens on vsock port 2222 and forwards to SSH on the guest IPv6 loopback. Port 22 is a privileged vsock port, and Fedora 45 sshd already binds it natively.
+- The host entry ssh phial reaches the guest with no network device.
+- Only the proxy port and the two agent sockets reach the guest. The guest units for the agent sockets stay down until a grant opens.
+- The guest listeners bind the IPv6 loopback first. The guest hosts file maps host.internal to ::1 first and to 127.0.0.1 as the fallback. The IPv4 rule stays local to the alias.
+
+Tests: ssh phial works during the install and after it. The proxy port answers from the guest at http://host.internal:4000.
+
+## Shares acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "a host file appears in the guest with no restart".
+
+- A host file written to the hostshare appears in the guest at /mnt/hostshare with no restart.
+- A guest file written to /mnt/hostshare appears in the host directory.
+- The secrets share mounts ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec. A write to it fails with Read-only file system. An execute attempt fails with Permission denied.
+- The file mode is 0600 and the owner is phial:phial in the guest. sudo -u nobody cat of the file fails with Permission denied.
+
+virtiofsd runs one unit per share with sockets in the runtime directory. The explicit uid and gid map is :1000:1000:1 on both sides, pinned by the kickstart uid and gid.
+
+## Secrets store acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "the list command prints names and never values".
+
+- phial ls prints the names, modes, sources, and upstreams. It never prints a value. The test suite asserts this on a mapping with known values.
+- One row resolves through keepassxc:// over the Secret Service. The entry attribute phial carries the key.
+- Env rows land as one file per secret in the secrets share, named after the secret.
+- Proxy rows keep the value on the host. The guest holds the placeholder phial-proxy-placeholder.
+- Every value is written with a temp file and a rename. The Go CLI uses renameio.
+
+## Grants acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "the secret file disappears on time with no other step".
+
+- phial allow --for 6s writes the secret file and the grant file. The file disappears at expiry with no other step. The timer is a transient systemd timer created over the user manager D-Bus with 1 second accuracy.
+- phial revoke removes both files and cancels the timer.
+- phial status reads the expiry and prints the remaining time.
+- The desktop notification fires on grant and on revoke over the org.freedesktop.Notifications D-Bus API.
+
+Grant subject types are api-key, ssh-key, and gpg-key. An api-key grant covers one mapping row. An ssh-key grant loads one key file from the keys directory. A gpg-key grant opens the GnuPG window.
+
+## CLI and runner acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "the guest runner prints the variable in the guest".
+
+- phial run loads every file in /mnt/phial-secrets as an environment variable and runs the command. In the guest it prints the granted value.
+- The path unit phial-secrets.path restarts the consumer units when the share changes. A phial sync on the host restarts the guest consumer within one second.
+- A consumer unit loads its secret with LoadCredential= and reads it from the credential directory.
+
+The CLI is one statically linked Go binary with the subcommands sync, allow, revoke, status, ls, and run. run is the guest runner. The binary builds with CGO_ENABLED=0 and installs to the user path on both sides.
+
+The test suite tests/test-cli.sh runs 22 tests against the binary. All 22 pass. It covers ls, sync, both modes, and the runner. It also covers allow and revoke, the timed expiry, and the proxy injection and stripping. It also covers the 403 and 404 responses and the ssh-key load and drop.
+
+## Agent forwarding acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "a guest push prompts on the host, and the guest holds no private key".
+
+- The dedicated ssh-agent runs on the host with its socket in the runtime directory. phial allow --kind ssh-key loads the key with ssh-add -c -t 1h semantics, which is confirm-before-use and a one hour lifetime.
+- The agent socket forwards to the guest over vsock. The guest sees the key with ssh-add -l and holds no private key file.
+- A signing attempt from the guest pops the GTK askpass on the host with the key fingerprint and Allow and Deny buttons. The dialog was confirmed on the host during validation.
+- The GnuPG extra socket forwards over vsock. The guest gpg-agent socket answers with the host GnuPG version 2.5.24 in restricted mode. The pinentry prompt runs on the host through pinentry-gnome3.
+
+The forwards open only while a grant is open. When the grant expires, the revoke stops the forwards on both sides.
+
+## Proxy acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "revoking the grant returns 403 on the next request".
+
+- Caddy runs as a user unit with admin off and listens on the IPv6 loopback port 4000.
+- phial sync rewrites the main Caddyfile and writes one drop-in per proxy row. The change applies with systemctl --user reload phial-caddy. Caddy 2.11.7 reloads on SIGUSR1. The CLI falls back to a restart on older builds.
+- The drop-in matches the grant file for the row before it injects the secret into the Authorization header. A missing grant file returns 403. An unmatched path returns 404.
+- From the guest: no grant returns 403 and a grant returns 200. On a grant the real secret reaches the upstream and the row prefix is stripped. Revoking returns 403 on the next request.
+
+## Display and audio acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "gnome-shell runs on the virtio GPU, and one GL app appears through waypipe".
+
+- gnome-shell runs on the virtio GPU. The journal shows the GBM renderer on /dev/dri/card1 with the virtio_gpu driver, and gnome-shell selects the boot VGA GPU as primary.
+- One GL app appears through waypipe. gnome-text-editor from the guest opened a window on the host desktop through waypipe over vsock. The window was seen during validation.
+
+The display paths: phial-waypipe runs one guest app over waypipe on vsock port 5553 with the guest display 0. phial-rdp connects to the full session over RDP on port 3389 through the vsock port forward. FreeRDP authentication against the gnome-remote-desktop system daemon succeeds with /auth-only through the forward. Audio and clipboard travel over RDP.
+
+The virtio GPU 3D path is broken on this host stack and is documented in docs/TOOLCHAIN.md. This validation used PHIAL_GPU=virgl. The default PHIAL_GPU=venus keeps blob, venus, and the native context on.
+
+## Networking acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "a scan from the guest sees the LAN, and mDNS resolves both ways".
+
+- The bridge phialbr0 carries the Ethernet port and the tap phialtap0. The guest gets LAN reachability through the bridge and pings the LAN gateway over IPv6 with 1 ms round trip.
+- The passt fallback route has the highest guest route metric. The guest shows two default routes with metrics 100 for the LAN and 1000 for the passt NIC.
+- mDNS resolves both ways. The host resolves phial.local to the guest LAN address and the guest resolves the host name to its LAN address. Avahi runs on both sides and the bridge carries the multicast.
+- IPv6 runs first on every interface. The dual stack stays as the fallback.
+
+The USB Wi-Fi passthrough line is ready in the launcher variables but untested, because no USB Wi-Fi adapter exists yet.
+
+## Hardware passthrough acceptance
+
+The acceptance reads "three unplug and replug cycles return every device to the guest".
+
+- usb-host passes the named devices by vendor and product ID. The udev rules grant the host user access with uaccess.
+- Three unplug and replug cycles ran with the host device authorize file. Every cycle returned all five devices to the guest with their drivers bound: ftdi_sio, cdc_acm, uvcvideo and snd-usb-audio, usbhid, and cdc_ncm.
+- The second xHCI controller passes with vfio-pci on its whole IOMMU group. The guest sees the native 12 port xHCI with the real hub topology and every device on the hub chain. The internal camera, fingerprint reader, Bluetooth, and WWAN on that controller travel with it, as expected.
+- The launcher refuses to bind the only xHCI controller. The guard counts xHCI controllers and stops when one remains.
+- The restore returns both group members to their host drivers, xhci_hcd and intel_pmc_ssram_telemetry.
+
+One fix came out of this phase: /dev/vfio group nodes need a udev uaccess rule, which the root bundle now writes.
+
+## Performance
+
+The guest runs with 11 vCPUs in one socket with threads=1 and 15 GiB RAM on a shared memfd with huge pages. The disk is a raw image with preallocation=falloc, cache=none, aio=native, and discard=unmap behind an iothread with num-queues=4 pinned to the last CPU. The iothread pin runs after QEMU starts. No GPU passes through. Measurement with perf and perf kvm remains a follow-up.
+
+## Guest lifecycle
+
+The guest installs once from the kickstart and updates through Ansible as one pet machine. No image is published and no base image is built. The Ansible layout follows vanlug-infrastructure with playbooks, group_vars, and shared configuration. It uses kickstart instead of cloud-init.
+
+## Agent skills
+
+The skill set installs to ~/.agents/skills/phial/SKILL.md in the guest through Ansible. It names the launcher, the runner, the proxy, and both display paths. It tells the agent that the secrets must stay secret and that the agent must never print a value. It points at the serial console for kernel work. The sync and grant commands stay host only.
+
+The remaining gap: the skill cannot stop a determined agent from reading the secrets share. The share is read-only and the runner holds the values in the environment by design.
+
+## Items that need the user
+
+- KeePassXC must be open and unlocked on the host for keepassxc:// lookups. The validation used the host Secret Service provider, which answers the same standard API.
+- The MOK enrollment flow is in place with mokutil at boot or virt-fw-vars while the VM is down. No custom module needed enrollment in this session, so the live enrollment is untested. virt-fw-vars --help lists the DER enrollment flags.
+- The USB Wi-Fi adapter passthrough waits for hardware.
+
+## Application acceptance, 2026-10-09
+
+The window drives every page and every action. The test script tests/ui-test.sh starts the application, waits for its bus name, and captures seven screenshots. Run it with make ui-test.
+
+- The sidebar carries three pages. The pages are Machines, Secrets, and Requests.
+- Every page opens. The detail page pushes onto the content navigation view, so the sidebar stays and the swipe gesture works.
+- The phone width collapses the split view. Selecting a sidebar row shows the content. The header arrow returns to the sidebar.
+- The application survives six navigation cycles with no panic.
+
+## Install acceptance, 2026-10-09
+
+A full install of Fedora Workstation 45 ran from the application. The log reports "phial install finished" with "the guest installed and rebooted".
+
+- The install reports seven steps through vm.installStatus.
+- The download reports bytes. The package step reports the current package and the count.
+- The console marker "Restarting system" marks the last step done.
+- Cancel Install stops the installer and removes the partial disk. This was tested on a live install.
+- The installed machine then reports the Stopped state in the window.
+
+Three defects appeared during this test and are fixed.
+
+- The kickstart carried no install source. Anaconda reported "Kickstart insufficient". Install now writes a url line into the kickstart.
+- A 0-byte disk file skipped allocation. The installer reported "No usable disks". The launcher now treats an empty file as absent.
+- The launcher waited for the swtpm and virtiofsd sockets without starting their units. The units now declare RuntimeDirectory, and the launcher starts them.
+
+## Stability acceptance, 2026-10-09
+
+The application crashed after a few minutes with "purego: the maximum number of callbacks has been reached". The page refreshers rebuilt their whole page on every tick, and every rebuild held new callbacks. The refreshers now run only when their data changes. A six-cycle navigation test then keeps the application alive.
